# McCore Task System

## CoreTask Variants

All tasks extend `CoreTask` and are executed via the Bukkit scheduler. Each variant adds a specific capability.

| Variant | Use when you need |
|---------|------------------|
| `CoreTask` | A one-shot task with no extra behavior |
| `DelayableCoreTask` | A one-shot task that starts after a tick delay |
| `CancelableCoreTask` | A repeating task that can be cancelled externally |
| `RepeatableCoreTask` | A task that repeats on a fixed interval (extends `CancelableCoreTask`) |
| `ExpireableCoreTask` | A repeating task that auto-cancels after a time limit or a maximum number of intervals |
| `MultiExecutionCoreTask` | A one-shot task designed to be `runTask()`-ed multiple times (tracks execution count) |

## Code Templates

### One-shot sync task

```java
new CoreTask(plugin) {
    @Override
    public void run() {
        // executes once on the main thread
    }
}.runTask();
```

### Delayed async task

```java
new DelayableCoreTask(plugin, delayTicks) {
    @Override
    public void run() {
        // executes once after delayTicks, off the main thread
    }
}.runTask(true); // true = async
```

### Repeating task (cancellable)

```java
CancelableCoreTask task = new RepeatableCoreTask(plugin, delayTicks, periodTicks) {
    @Override
    public void run() {
        // executes every periodTicks, starting after delayTicks
    }
};
task.runTask(); // sync; pass true for async
// Later: task.cancelTask();
```

### Expireable task — auto-cancel after duration

```java
new ExpireableCoreTask(plugin, delayTicks, maxDurationSeconds) {
    @Override
    public void run() {
        // repeats every tick until maxDurationSeconds elapses
    }
}.runTask();
```

### Expireable task — auto-cancel after N intervals

```java
new ExpireableCoreTask(plugin, delayTicks, periodTicks, maxIntervals) {
    @Override
    public void run() {
        // repeats up to maxIntervals times
    }
}.runTask();
```

### Multi-execution task

```java
MultiExecutionCoreTask task = new MultiExecutionCoreTask(plugin) {
    @Override
    public void run() {
        // body runs once per runTask() call
        // task.getExecutions() tracks how many times it has been run
    }
};
task.runTask();
// Later, run again:
task.runTask();
```

Use this when you need to re-trigger the same task logic without constructing a new object each time.

## Task State

All `CoreTask` instances track:
- `isTaskExecuted()` — whether `run()` has been called at least once
- `isAsync()` — whether the task runs off the main thread
- `getStartTime()` — `Instant` when the task was scheduled

Prefer calling `isTaskExecuted()` over maintaining your own boolean flag.

## Player Lifecycle Tasks

McCore provides two built-in tasks for player lifecycle management:

| Task | When it fires | Purpose |
|------|--------------|---------|
| `PlayerLoadTask` | Player join | Loads player data from DB into `CorePlayer`; fires `PlayerLoadEvent` when done |
| `PlayerUnloadTask` | Player quit | Saves player data to DB; fires `PlayerUnloadEvent`; removes from `PlayerManager` |

**Do not duplicate player load/unload logic inline in Bukkit listeners.** Use the events fired by these tasks:

```java
@EventHandler
public void onPlayerLoad(PlayerLoadEvent event) {
    // Safe — player data is fully loaded at this point
    McRPGPlayer player = (McRPGPlayer) event.getCorePlayer();
}
```

## Seconds vs Ticks

`DelayableCoreTask` and `ExpireableCoreTask` accept delay/period in **seconds as `double`**, not ticks. Internally the value is converted to ticks (20 ticks/second). Keep this in mind when reading constructor arguments.

`RepeatableCoreTask` uses ticks directly for both delay and period — check the constructor signature carefully.

## Anti-Patterns

- **No mutable plugin-level state on task objects** — a task object may be garbage-collected after it runs; don't rely on field values persisting across executions unless you manage the reference yourself
- **No `Thread.sleep()` inside `run()`** — use `DelayableCoreTask` instead
- **No Bukkit API calls from async tasks** — if a task runs async, schedule back to the main thread via `Bukkit.getScheduler().runTask(plugin, ...)` before touching entities, worlds, or inventories
- **No anonymous subclasses with captured mutable closures** — capture only effectively-final references; mutable state should live on the task object itself

